references/bpc/bathrooms-and-wet-areas/fold-down-grab-bar-specification.md:33:### best_practice_synthesis
references/bpc/communication-and-alerts/assistive-listening-systems.md:13:**Best-practice provision:**
references/bpc/communication-and-alerts/assistive-listening-systems.md:15:- Room loop (A-11): all assembly spaces ≥25 capacity (UK/ISO baseline); all ERP cat. 1–4 and spaces ≥50 seats (FR CEREMA — stricter best practice)
references/bpc/communication-and-alerts/assistive-listening-systems.md:18:**Opus 4.6 synthesis note (2026-03-29):** Synthesis reviewed and confirmed. Two findings. (1) The Japan gap (GAP-ALS-01) is the most significant jurisdiction finding: Japan is the only major jurisdiction with no mandatory hearing loop provision. This should be explicitly noted in A-11 as a jurisdiction-specific gap, not silently omitted. The guidebook's specification should state the IEC 60118-4 requirement as universal best practice while noting Japan's voluntary-only status. (2) The Auracast transition is correctly handled as dual-provision DAR. The critical design decision is: Auracast infrastructure (conduit + power) must be specified at DD stage even though IEC 60118-17 is not published. The cost of retrofit conduit after construction is 10–40× the cost of DD-stage provision. This is a DAR principle application — cross-reference Part 10 §10.1. The CEREMA trigger threshold (≥50 seats + all ERP cat. 1–4) is more comprehensive than the guidebook's ≥25 capacity. Recommend adopting the CEREMA threshold as the guidebook's best-practice standard. Evidence confidence: HIGH for loop provisions (IEC 60118-4, Tier 4, universally adopted); MODERATE for Auracast provisions (no IEC standard until 2027; product-stage evidence only).
references/bpc/communication-and-alerts/assistive-listening-systems.md:21:- Trigger threshold: guidebook ≥25 capacity vs FR CEREMA ≥50 seats + all ERP cat. 1–4. CEREMA is more comprehensive; flag as cross-jurisdictional best practice in A-11.
references/bpc/communication-and-alerts/deaf-acoustic-built-environment.md:8:**KNOWN CONFLICT (Q24, `workplan/ratification-execution-register-2026-07-13.md`):** this file's "STI ≥0.50 minimum; ≥0.60 best practice" framing conflicts with `room-acoustic-performance.md`'s "STI ≥0.60 general / ≥0.75 DEAF-CI" and `acoustics-speech-intelligibility-disability.md`'s flat "STI ≥0.5". Do not resolve by inspection or point-fix ahead of reverification — all three are in the same PRE-REHABILITATION cohort above; flagged for Phase E.2g to adjudicate together.
references/bpc/communication-and-alerts/deaf-acoustic-built-environment.md:28:### Best-practice synthesis
references/bpc/communication-and-alerts/deaf-acoustic-built-environment.md:30:**Most inclusive provision:** Hearing loop systems compliant with IEC 60118-4:2014+AMD1:2017 (field strength ±3 dB across listening plane, SNR ≥47 dB(A) above magnetic noise floor) to be installed in all assembly, reception, service counter, and primary communication spaces. RT60 in speech communication spaces not to exceed 0.4 s at 500 Hz–4 kHz (DIN 18041:2016 Nutzungsart A3/A4; BS 8300-2:2018 §11). Background noise criterion NC-25 or below in hearing-critical spaces. STI ≥ 0.60 in all looped spaces. Auracast (Bluetooth LE Audio) infrastructure provision to be included in all new assembly spaces as a DAR provision for technology transition. [IEC 60118-17 expected late 2027 — confirmed March 2026; dual provision best practice during transition.]
references/bpc/communication-and-alerts/deaf-acoustic-built-environment.md:34:**Conflict resolution:** IEC 60118-4 requires hearing loop provision; Auracast standard expected 2027 will complement, not replace, loops during transition. Best practice is dual provision: loop + Auracast-ready infrastructure. Loop decommissioning before Auracast is fully adopted would eliminate access for the majority of hearing aid users who lack Auracast-compatible devices.
references/bpc/communication-and-alerts/deaf-acoustic-built-environment.md:36:**Cross-language convergence:** RT60 ≤ 0.4 s is the consensus best-practice target across all 14 languages and their governing standards (DIN 18041, BS 8300, BBR, NS 8175, BR18, Asetus 241, CTE HR, NBR 10152, NEN 9120 equivalent, Korean school standard). IEC 60118-4 is universally adopted as the hearing loop performance standard across all jurisdictions — the highest cross-language convergence of any DEAF-related specification.
references/bpc/communication-and-alerts/deaf-acoustic-built-environment.md:47:| RT60 ≤ 0.4 s in speech communication spaces (best practice) | EN, SV, NO, DA, FI, FR, DE, NL, ES, PT, KO, IT | Tier 4–5 |
references/bpc/communication-and-alerts/deaf-acoustic-built-environment.md:48:| STI ≥ 0.50 minimum; ≥ 0.60 best practice | EN, FR, DE (DIN 18041) | Tier 4 |
references/bpc/communication-and-alerts/deaf-classroom-reverberation-time.md:10:### Best-practice synthesis
references/bpc/communication-and-alerts/deaf-spatial-design.md:26:### Best-practice synthesis
references/bpc/controls-and-hardware/reach-range-and-accessible-controls.md:16:### Best-practice synthesis
references/bpc/controls-and-hardware/reach-range-and-accessible-controls.md:25:**Conflict resolution:** The ADA side-reach maximum was 1370mm (1991 ADAAG); LPA advocacy and ANSI A117.1 lowered it to 1220mm. This is a Co-1-driven improvement. Best practice adopts 1220mm as the absolute maximum regardless of reach type. Lower (1100mm) is best practice for the most constrained users. The NBR 9050 lateral reach-with-trunk-displacement envelope extends to higher values but this represents functional compensation, not design intent — design should eliminate the need for trunk displacement.
references/bpc/controls-and-hardware/reach-range-and-accessible-controls.md:27:**Door hardware:** Lever handle or U-shaped pull mandatory. Round door knobs prohibited (require wrist twist — functionally inaccessible for significant proportion of UPL and arthritis population). Automatic door openers best practice for all entrance doors.
references/bpc/controls-and-hardware/reach-range-and-accessible-controls.md:42:| Switches/sockets 450–1200mm; best practice 450–1050mm | UK (BS 8300) | EN | 5 |
references/bpc/controls-and-hardware/reach-range-and-accessible-controls.md:53:| Door force max | CA: ≤38N | ADA: no max specified (fire doors excepted) | Regulatory gap in US; CA best practice fills it |
references/bpc/cross-population/cross-population-conflict-resolutions.md:15:### Best-practice synthesis
references/bpc/cross-population/cross-population-conflict-resolutions.md:57:- Cost: 2440mm primary corridor uses more floor area than 1800mm. This is a Population-Mode trade-off (best practice vs code floor). Cost-driven reductions are Mode-6 / Tier-7 arguments, not best-practice arguments.
